WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.548
Last video for week six, and the one that frames your decision memo.

2
00:00:04.598 --> 00:00:09.500
The executive reading it has three options: build the agent, buy one, or wait.

3
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:16.222
This video gives you a way to lay out that choice, and two cautionary tales about depending on platforms.

4
00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:19.028
Lay the three options out honestly.

5
00:00:19.078 --> 00:00:27.804
Build means you control the tools, the data and the dial, and you own the evaluation and the fixes, paid for in engineers and time.

6
00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:40.442
Buy is faster to start, and the vendor's evaluation is not yours; your data goes where the vendor runs it, and the question to ask any vendor is the pass rate on your cases, not theirs.

7
00:00:40.492 --> 00:00:45.357
Wait is a real option, because capability keeps improving, but waiting is not free.

8
00:00:45.407 --> 00:00:48.516
It forfeits the value and, more importantly, the learning.

9
00:00:48.566 --> 00:00:52.729
A small pilot that measures carefully is the good way to wait.

10
00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:54.672
Two cautionary tales.

11
00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:55.916
First, platform risk.

12
00:00:55.966 --> 00:01:01.719
OpenAI launched a visual agent builder in October 2025 as part of its AgentKit.

13
00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:13.326
In June 2026 it announced that the visual builder would be retired, with a final date of November 30, 2026, and pointed users to the code-first SDK instead.

14
00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:15.813
Thirteen months from launch to retirement.

15
00:01:15.863 --> 00:01:29.492
If your decision memo recommends buying, say what happens to the service when the platform changes its mind, because the odds that it will during the life of the service are not small.

16
00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:31.269
Second, agent washing.

17
00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:49.582
When Gartner predicted in June 2025 that more than 40 percent of agentic AI projects would be canceled by the end of 2027, it also estimated that only a small fraction of the vendors selling agentic AI had a genuine agent, as opposed to a rebranded chatbot or a scripted automation.

18
00:01:49.632 --> 00:01:52.454
You know how to tell the difference now.

19
00:01:52.504 --> 00:01:56.045
Ask to see the loop, the tools and a trace.

20
00:01:56.095 --> 00:01:58.918
Ask for pass rates on a golden set.

21
00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:05.381
A vendor who cannot show you a trace is selling you a dial position they have not earned.

22
00:02:06.131 --> 00:02:09.041
Then the return on investment, with measured numbers.

23
00:02:09.091 --> 00:02:24.211
Benefits: minutes saved per task, times tasks per month, times the loaded hourly cost of the person who does it today; faster cycle time, if someone will actually pay for speed; and errors avoided, priced at what an error costs today.

24
00:02:24.261 --> 00:02:36.790
Costs: cost per successful task from your traces, human review and approval time priced in minutes, and the build and run costs, which include the people, the licenses, the regression run and the dashboard.

25
00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:47.520
If the benefit depends on the agent being unattended and your pass^k does not support unattended, the memo says so and prices the gated version instead.

26
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.817
So the decision memo is two pages.

27
00:02:50.867 --> 00:03:00.093
Page one: the job, the agent, and the numbers, pass^k, cost per successful task, latency, and the dial position you are recommending.

28
00:03:00.143 --> 00:03:04.917
Page two: the risks and controls, the owner and approvers, and the recommendation.

29
00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:07.143
Deploy, extend the pilot, or stop.

30
00:03:07.193 --> 00:03:10.853
Say which, and say what evidence would change your mind.

31
00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:13.079
An executive can act on that.

32
00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:20.871
Next week, the last set of videos: multi-agent systems, long-running agents, and what the benchmarks say about where the limits are.
